📡 FM Radio Streaming System – Full Architecture & Developer Reference

A complete guide to building a cloud-based FM radio streaming system with:

Browser-based admin live (WebRTC → SRS)

Auto music playback (Liquidsoap → SRS)

Secure token-based publishing (Node.js backend)

HLS streaming for web & mobile listeners

SRS http_hooks for authentication

Failover (admin → auto music)

This document explains everything you need to remember and reference.

🧱 1. High-Level System Overview

The platform supports:

Admin speaking live from browser (WebRTC)

Automatic music playback when admin is offline

Playback for listeners on web or mobile via HLS

Secure publishing using backend-controlled tokens

Stream switching handled by Liquidsoap fallback logic

🏛️ 2. Core Components
Component	Purpose
SRS (Simple Realtime Server)	Accepts WebRTC/RTMP input, outputs HLS/WebRTC/FLV
Liquidsoap	Auto DJ engine, random playlists, scheduling, fallback logic
Node.js Backend	Auth, issue stream tokens, validate SRS hooks
React Admin Panel	Admin “Go Live” WebRTC interface
React / Mobile App	Listener UI, plays HLS stream
Redis/DB	Store live state, schedule, tokens
🖧 3. Streaming Flow Architecture
Admin Browser (WebRTC)
        │
        ▼
      SRS (Ingest)
   - WebRTC receive
   - RTMP receive (auto music)
   - HLS output
        │
        ▼
 Liquidsoap (Auto DJ)
        │
        ▼
      SRS
        │
        ▼
Listeners (Web/Mobile)
        │
        ▼
HLS Player (m3u8)


Admin live audio comes from WebRTC.

Auto music comes from Liquidsoap.

Liquidsoap can also handle stream switching.

SRS outputs a single final HLS URL for listeners.

🔐 4. Security Flow (Prevent Unauthorized Publishing)

SRS uses http_hooks to validate publishing:

Admin → SRS → Backend /api/srs/on-publish → Allow/Deny

SRS calls backend every time someone tries to publish:
{
  "action": "on_publish",
  "app": "live",
  "stream": "admin",
  "param": "?token=XYZ"
}


Backend returns:

{ "code": 0 }  → allow
{ "code": 1 }  → deny

🧩 5. SRS Server Configuration Explained
listen 1935;                # RTMP port
http_server {               # HLS & static files
    enabled on;
    listen 8080;
    dir ./objs/nginx/html;
}

http_api {                  # WebRTC signaling API
    enabled on;
    listen 1985;
}

rtc_server {                # WebRTC media transport
    enabled on;
    listen 8000;
    candidate YOUR_PUBLIC_IP;
}

vhost __defaultVhost__ {

    rtmp { enabled on; }    # Allow RTMP ingest (auto DJ)
    rtc  { enabled on; }    # Allow WebRTC ingest/play

    hls {
        enabled on;
        hls_fragment 3;
        hls_window 30;
        hls_path ./objs/nginx/html;
    }

    # Security hooks
    http_hooks {
        enabled on;
        on_publish   http://backend:3000/api/srs/on-publish;
        on_unpublish http://backend:3000/api/srs/on-unpublish;
    }
}

What this does:

Accept WebRTC input on webrtc://.../live/admin

Accept RTMP input on rtmp://.../live/music

Output HLS on http://server:8080/live/*.m3u8

Ask backend on every publish attempt (“Allow or deny?”)

🎤 6. Admin “Go Live” Workflow (WebRTC)
Step-by-step:

Admin logs in to React admin panel

Frontend requests:

GET /api/live/publish-config


Backend verifies admin → returns:

{
  "streamUrl": "webrtc://server/live/admin?token=SIGNED_JWT",
  "srsApiUrl": "http://server:1985/rtc/v1/publish/"
}


Frontend uses getUserMedia({ audio:true })

Frontend sends SDP offer to SRS via /rtc/v1/publish/

SRS calls backend /api/srs/on-publish

Backend validates token → allow/deny

If allowed:

WebRTC stream flows to SRS

SRS generates:

http://server:8080/live/admin.m3u8

🎵 7. Auto Music Flow (Liquidsoap)

Liquidsoap generates continuous audio:

music = playlist("/radio/music")
random_music = random(weights=[1], [music])

output.rtmp(
   url="rtmp://server/live/music",
   random_music
)

Using fallback logic:
admin = input.rtmp("rtmp://server/live/admin")
music = playlist("/radio/music")

radio = fallback([admin, music])

output.rtmp("rtmp://server/live/radio", radio)


Listeners use:

http://server:8080/live/radio.m3u8


Liquidsoap automatically switches:

Admin live → send admin

Admin offline → send music

📱 8. Listener App (Web/Mobile)

Frontend periodically checks:

GET /api/live/state


Returns:

{
  "isLive": true,
  "streamUrl": "http://server:8080/live/radio.m3u8"
}


Player:

<audio controls autoplay src="http://server:8080/live/radio.m3u8"></audio>


Works on:

Web

Android

iOS

React Native (via hls support)

🧲 9. How Unauthorized Access Is Prevented

☑ Server never trusts clients directly
☑ Admin must login to get a stream token
☑ WebRTC publish requires token
☑ SRS calls backend for validation
☑ Backend checks token, expiry, permissions
☑ Only backend can authorize publish
☑ Attackers cannot:

Guess a valid token

Publish without backend approval

Use expired tokens

📦 10. Useful Additional SRS Configurations
a. on_play / on_stop hooks (optional)
on_play  http://backend:3000/api/srs/on-play
on_stop  http://backend:3000/api/srs/on-stop


Use for:

Listener counting

Subscription-only streams

Analytics

b. Recording streams (DVR support)
dvr {
    enabled on;
    dvr_path ./objs/nginx/html/dvr;
    dvr_plan session;
    dvr_apply all;
}

c. Cleanup old HLS segments
hls_cleanup on;

d. Larger RTMP chunk size
rtmp {
    enabled on;
    chunk_size 60000;
}

